  {
    "episode_id": 91655,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91655.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think that depending on where you come from, you're going to look at this differently. We're in Monmouth right now. We have a Smithfield plant, not too far from here. And I think if you are a part of whether it's JBS or Tyson or Smithfield, you're going to see this differently. And I don't think there was any level of excitement about President Biden's news yesterday. Now, if you are someone who is interested in going into kind of an independent meatpacking business, if you are someone who raises cattle or pigs, you're probably going to think this is good news. The Biden administration is setting aside a billion dollars, and that is money from the American Rescue Plan. And what that will do is expand independent meat processing capacity. They've got that broken down into different categories. They have money to make sure that there are workers who can work in these independent meat packing, you know, basically fill the pipeline of workers. There's about 375 million of it will be financing for independent processing plants. And it also will allow for the producers actually to have relationships with these independent processors. All of this came out of you. And you remember the stories we would hear, especially from the pork producers. I remember talking with some pork producers who were very, very emotional when they couldn't find places to take their pigs at the height of the pandemic. So the part of this is to address that. and that there's a new lane that will be created for independent processors. And so I think that's probably the best way to look at it. So we've seen consolidation over the years where 85% of the meat processing is done by four corporations, and this is a way to expand and have more competition.",
