    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Okay, the biggest question will be how to fill the jobs in whether it's an independent meatpacking plant or the current processing plants already in place throughout the United States. They've been struggling, as you know, through the pandemic to get employees.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The biggest question is how to fill jobs at either an independent meatpacking plant or existing U.S. processing plants.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Meatpacking plants in the U.S. have struggled to hire employees during the pandemic.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Job vacancies in meatpacking are a significant problem requiring urgent attention.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Existing processing plants and new independent ones face similar labor challenges.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker and listener share common knowledge about the impact of the pandemic on employment in meatpacking.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Labor shortages in meatpacking affect both operational continuity and broader economic stability.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a shared understanding that filling these positions is feasible through strategic planning.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 91655,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91655.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "I think part of it is early in the pandemic, there were a lot of meat processors, those who were working in that business who were getting sick. And we had some folks who were working who passed away. And I think there was obviously a level of scare of going into work. When I look at someone, we have a Tyson plant in the Quad Cities in Jazlyn. We have the Smithfield plant here. And what I will say about both of the management of those companies is they did react. They did make some adjustments. They put up these dividers between workers. They put out picnic tables so people could go outside on their breaks. They spread people out more. I think that they were doing their best to address something that was, you know, being part of a world pandemic is something that none of us had experienced in our lifetimes. And we were in regular communication with the Smithfield people and the Tyson people. And I know they did their best to make some adjustments as part of that. That said, they also have had some incentive financial incentives. which helped keep people on the job. But I think as we look forward, Vanessa, there are a lot of different lines of work now that the minimum wage is going to go up, whether it's regulated or not. In Illinois, the minimum wage now is $12 that went into effect on January 1st. But I think there's a lot of companies that are saying, hey, we're going to have to raise our rates in order to fill these job openings that we have. And I think just by the natural business process that's going to happen in order to fill jobs.",
