    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91655.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Well, last time you and I spoke, I was a little bit newer as chair of the General Farm Commodities and Risk Management Subcommittee of the House Ag Committee. We had a series of hearings over the past year to listen to our family farmers and figure out what do we need to know as we lay the foundation for the 2023 Farm Bill. I will not be a member of Congress after January 3rd of 2023, but I am still in a position as chair of the subcommittee to help lay that foundation. So as we look ahead for the year, we are going to do a series of listening sessions and hearings and we're actually going to do field hearings where we will go out to different parts of the country and listen to growers and producers about and ask these questions open end questions what do you want to see in the next farm bill what is working in the current farm bill and what is not working what adjustments do we need to make As we look at what's happening with the climate, whether it's the direction that came through, whether it's the droughts in so many areas that experienced drought this past year, flooding that we've experienced in previous years, we know that risk management will be very, very important to get right. And so that's the, since that's the subcommittee I chair, we'll do a lot of focus on that as well. My goal will just be to have good policy, a good policy foundation laid, and then that will play into what the 2023 Farm Bill will end up looking like.",
