  {
    "episode_id": 91655,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91655.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Introduce that in September. We have bipartisan support of that. That is where we would require E-20 to be used in the coming few years and then that move up to E-30 by I think it's 20-31 and also what would require our automobile, our vehicle manufacturers to make their engines so it can burn higher octane, the E20 and the E30. And Vanessa, I don't know if I talked with you about this on your show, but it was out of your ag round table that that idea came up for the legislation that we ended up writing the Next Generation Fuels Act. I hadn't thought about the vehicle manufacturing part of it. And if your listeners will remember when you did that round table and I was one of your guests, that came up and I said, well let me look into that and figure out what we can do. So as a result of your reporting and your ag round table, we took that and included that as part of our Next Generation Fuels Act. So thank you to you and the folks who were sitting around the table that day who brought this up. And to my surprise, we actually have the vehicle manufacturers, two of the major vehicle manufacturers who are supportive of this. And so we've really gotten some unbelievable endorsements, whether it's the corn growers, the soybean association, but we have some great support for this legislation. And let me just like to manage expectations. It is just really, we've got to get buy-in for members of Congress and senators who don't come from farm country on something like this. And that, frankly, has been the challenge, is we've got to get more support. So that's where the national organizations can play into this, to lobby their lawmakers who maybe don't come from farm country or from states that aren't heavily ag like the state of Illinois. So we need to continue to grow our support and I'm hoping we'll be able to move this along.",
