  {
    "episode_id": 91655,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91655.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "One of the top priorities has to be the fact that we passed major legislation to get high speed internet to every corner of this country. We have to execute on that now. So in our congressional district, which includes Monmouth and Warren County and 13 other counties going all the way up to the Wisconsin state line over to the Mississippi River and then into Central Illinois and Peoria, We've got one in four people don't have access to high speed internet and whether you want to look at things like telehealth or our kids learning or small business owners or our family farmers that is something that will be critical to get to get taken care of. So we have a hundred million dollars that's coming to the state of Illinois and that will be enough to get us mapped out so where we have to actually pinpoint each and every home and each and every business that doesn't have access to high speed internet and then execute on delivering that. So we've got the money now which has frankly been the problem in the past. We haven't had the money to make sure that we have high speed internet every place. We do have the money now and we have to execute on that. So I would say that is a priority We are still looking at universal pre-kindergarten, so for our three and four-year-olds to have that part of public education. And I'm not sure if we're that's going to stand. That was part of this Build Back Better Act that has really kind of taken on a life of its own. I think that for those who have followed this issue, that is not going to pass in the form that President Biden initially laid out. I think what will happen is that will be broken into different pieces and we'll see if we can get enough votes to pass whether it's universal pre-kindergarten, whether it's making sure that child care is available in every community and is affordable for families, whether it's the child tax credit that expired at the end of the year. and continuing that and even things like rural broadband. I think what will happen is we will have that broken into different parts and some will pass and some won't have a shot at passing.",
